WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
VOICE OVER: ﻿Rubber is useful stuff.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.560
And if you take some rubber, and&nbsp;
reinforce it with rigid particles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:12.400
you can make it stiffer -&nbsp;
which can be even more useful.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:20.080
Things like tyres and belts and shock absorbers&nbsp;
all need stiff rubber that can resist deformation.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.640
This material property is known&nbsp;
as a high elastic modulus - while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.480
stretchier rubber would have a low modulus.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.440
It’s easy to increase the elastic&nbsp;
modulus by adding rigid particles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.400
but what’s harder is stopping stiff&nbsp;
rubber cracking under repeated stress.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:42.600
The ability of a material to resist cracks&nbsp;
under cyclical stress is known as its fatigue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:49.920
threshold. And the fatigue threshold of&nbsp;
rubber has stayed the same for decades.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.880
But now a team of researchers have&nbsp;
created a new type of rubber with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:59.920
a fatigue threshold 10 times higher than before.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.680
Traditional reinforced rubbers, or Particle&nbsp;
Reinforced Elastomers as they are known,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.920
are made up of networks of rigid&nbsp;
particles and crosslinked polymers.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.840
The crosslinks effectively weld&nbsp;
the polymer strands together

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:21.440
This makes the rubber stiffer,&nbsp;
but also more brittle.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.360
The new rubber is a little different. It’s&nbsp;
still made of polymers and reinforced with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:33.040
added rigid particles, but the polymers are&nbsp;
much longer with fewer crosslinks. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:39.040
these long strands are entangled with one&nbsp;
another - like a bowl of spaghetti, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:45.560
the remaining crosslinks locking this structure&nbsp;
in place and stopping the polymers coming apart.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:50.520
There’s a low friction between entangled&nbsp;
polymers which helps transmit any stress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:55.880
across the entire polymer chain. The stress&nbsp;
can also be transmitted from the polymers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:00.520
the rigid particles and from one rigid&nbsp;
particle to another. All of which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:06.720
that stress is no longer concentrated on a&nbsp;
single point, such as the edge of a crack.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:13.080
The combination of long polymer chains and rigid&nbsp;
particles work at different scales to amplify both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:20.160
the elastic modulus, and the fatigue threshold.&nbsp;
And that gives you a material that’s stiff and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:27.440
which can take more stress without cracks&nbsp;
forming or growing through multiple cycles

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.480
This development could have a huge impact for&nbsp;
manufacturing all sorts of existing products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:36.160
as well as in emerging fields like soft robotics.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:41.640
The researchers hope that this study could&nbsp;
even lay the groundwork for rubber that never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:52.600
wears out, opening an enormous space&nbsp;
for material design and applications.

